+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for lawyers in 2026?
+For broad usefulness, start with `CourtListener`, `Juriscraper`, `OpenContracts`, `LexNLP`, `docassemble`, and `DocuSeal`. Together they cover research, ingestion, extraction, workflow automation, and execution.
+
+### Are there truly free AI legal research tools on GitHub?
+Yes, but the strongest ones are often infrastructure rather than polished consumer apps. `CourtListener`, `OLDP`, `Korean Law MCP`, and `Pasal` are the best examples in this repo.
+
+### What is the best GitHub repository for open source contract review?
+`OpenContracts` is the most complete platform in this list. `LexNLP`, `ContraxSuite`, `ContextGem`, and `LegalBench-RAG` are also important depending on whether you need extraction, application logic, or evaluation.
+
+### Which open source tools help lawyers automate forms and filings?
+`docassemble`, `docassemble-AssemblyLine`, `ALToolbox`, and `docassemble-EFSPIntegration` are the best fit if your goal is guided intake, court-form automation, and e-filing.
+
+### Are there open source DocuSign alternatives suitable for legal teams?
+Yes. `DocuSeal` is the strongest fully open GitHub-native option here, and `OpenSign` is another serious public repository for e-signing workflows.
+
+### Can I self-host these legal AI tools for client-confidential work?
+Often yes, but you still need to review licenses, deployment docs, and data flows repo by repo. This list intentionally includes public GitHub repos so legal teams can inspect code and self-host where appropriate.
+
+### Why do some entries say `License: See repo`?
